/*  A program written across two files, holding a type that belongs to the other one.

    `charge` is declared inside the `bookkeeping` book in bookkeeping.cufe. Pulling the book
    brings the type into scope BY ITS SHORT NAME, for the length of the block — the same way
    `matrix` arrives with `Pull a book on collections.` Outside the block the name is gone.

    ★★ THE POINT OF THE PAIR IS WHO OWES THE DEPENDENCY, and it is worth reading beside
    basket.cufe. `bookkeeping`'s `rounded-total` names `math` WITHOUT pulling it, so the debt falls
    to whoever pulls `bookkeeping` — which is why this file has to open `Pull a book on math.`
    around it. `pennies` does the opposite: it is written inside the pull it needs, so it keeps it,
    and basket.cufe names `pennies` and nothing else. Both spellings are right, and the choice is
    about whether the dependency is the caller's business or an implementation detail.

    ★ The nesting is not ceremony: take the outer pull away and the program is refused AT THE
    PULL, naming what is missing, rather than dying part way through.

    ⚠ Both of these are BOOKS. Neither owns a lifetime, and that is the whole of what separates a
    book from a module — so there is no example here of the other kind, because a rabbit is the only
    thing that owns one and nobody can write another yet.  */

Pull a book on math.
    Pull a book on bookkeeping.
        Define spending as a series of charge with (
            a new charge { the note "coffee", the amount 3.5 },
            a new charge { the note "notebook", the amount 24.99 },
            a new charge { the note "train fare", the amount 8.256 }).

        For each item in spending, repeat:
            State cast item's line.
        Done.

        State "".
        State "exact:   {cast bookkeeping's total on (spending)}".
        State "rounded: {cast bookkeeping's rounded-total on (spending)}".
    Done.
Done.
